		<div class="post" id="post-2171771">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2171771)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2171771">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1417656186 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Dec 2014 01:23</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2171771">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;minor&nbsp;issues</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Manipulation of local economic infrastructure has been implemented to reduce residential and industrial population density in the immediate area of Containment Site 2377</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems somewhat excessive. The Foundation is literally depressing the local economy just to get a little more space around the house? Doesn't seem like a commensurate level of resources to fix a problem that could probably just be solved by passive surveillance and a sturdy fence. Also, I'm not sure &quot;infrastructure&quot; is the term you want here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Procurement of SCP-2377-1 was completed on 10/14/199█ via Shinjuku Complex Properties, a Foundation front company.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I get that this is something a lot of authors do. It also makes absolutely no sense that a clandestine agency would advertise itself in this manner.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to an inability to independently verify the veracity of events</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This part's redundant; you can just say &quot;verify&quot; here and it would be fine.</p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>The initial murdervision thing in the house concept is decent enough, and I think it could serve as a good anchor to an article. I also think your decision to flesh it out a bit with a story is a good one, as this would need a little more depth to it past the premise in order to be a fully-realized piece.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, the story that this has been given isn't really doing the article much good. When you introduce a new element to a narrative, it's okay to have it give the reader mere hints and suggestions to a larger narrative, but in this article the four people breaking into the house followed by cryptic message thing isn't really leading me anywhere. It's just sort of (from the reader's perspective) random mysterious stuff. Since it doesn't show any possible path(s) for the narrative to go further, it comes across to me as filler text; lengthening the story without really adding to it.</p>
<p>After reading your spoilers, I think what you're going for in the larger story could be interesting. But nothing that you said in your collapsible really came through to me in the story, and so the reading experience becomes dependent on an external source to carry the weight that you want to give it. I would recommend weaving a little more of those proposed story elements into the narrative, especially if your intention is to use this as the basis for further works.</p>

							</div>

								
					
