								<div class="title" id="post-title-2171964">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dillinger-phd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005876); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2005876&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607547052" alt="Dillinger PhD" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2005876)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dillinger-phd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005876); return false;" >Dillinger PhD</a></span> <span class="odate time_1417687314 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Dec 2014 10:01</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2171964">
									

<blockquote>
<p>This seems somewhat excessive. The Foundation is literally depressing the local economy just to get a little more space around the house? Doesn't seem like a commensurate level of resources to fix a problem that could probably just be solved by passive surveillance and a sturdy fence. Also, I'm not sure &quot;infrastructure&quot; is the term you want here.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Excessive? A bit, yes. This is a combination of developing my Foundation headcanon for their protocols (this originally included a poorly veiled reference to Detroit obsolescence) and vaguely hinting at this skip being considered higher priority than is let on elsewise.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I get that this is something a lot of authors do. It also makes absolutely no sense that a clandestine agency would advertise itself in this manner.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Some people like this shtick, some people don't. I'm the former; I also don't think this precludes the Foundation having fronts that <em>don't</em> fulfill the acronym gimmick.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This part's redundant; you can just say &quot;verify&quot; here and it would be fine.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Point taken, will be edited.</p>
<p>For the additional comments:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I feel like the reader is lead along enough here so it's fair to agree to disagree. The intruders are tied to the GOI by the interview, where it's also expressed that they wish to connect with the killer-POI. The killer-POI, in his writing of the note, expresses:</p>
<ul>
<li>A previously undocumented desire to communicate. Deviation in m.o. for serial killers is noteworthy.</li>
<li>Potential-bordering-on-probable understanding of being spectated, given that his murders have as of yet not been discovered elsewise. As-of-yet undetermined by Foundation whether, if this is the case, he is attempting to communicate to the GOI (more probable) or the Foundation.</li>
<li>Understanding of some fundamental belief in a similar way to the GOI.</li>
</ul>
<p>I think this develops a significant enough potential narrative path for the GOI, the POI, and their eventual direct connection.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I do appreciate your comments on this and the other piece I have up, even if we've not quite seen eye-to-eye.</p>

